# ============================================================================
# KiwiPilotPrep — environment
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit a filled-in .env: it is in .gitignore
# and must stay there. Every value below is read on the server only; nothing
# here except NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL reaches the browser.
# ============================================================================

# ---- Database --------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL 16+. In production, require TLS: append &sslmode=require
#
# Size the pool explicitly. Left unset, Prisma opens (cpus * 2 + 1) connections
# per instance — 33 on a 16-core box, measured — so three instances exhaust a
# default max_connections of 100 and the fourth cannot connect at all.
#
# Budget it:
#
#   instances x connection_limit  <=  max_connections
#                                     - superuser_reserved_connections (3)
#                                     - migrations (2)
#                                     - admin/psql and monitoring (~7)
#
# With max_connections=100 that leaves about 88 to share out. At the value
# below, eight instances fit with room to spare.
#
# Measured on one instance at 100 concurrent students, 600 requests:
#   limit 33 (default) -> 34 connections, p95 1026 ms
#   limit 20           -> 21 connections, p95 1069 ms
#   limit 10           -> 11 connections, p95 1160 ms, no pool timeouts
# The database is not the bottleneck (hot queries run in under 0.15 ms), so
# the smaller pool costs little and buys a great deal of headroom.
#
# pool_timeout is the seconds a request waits for a free connection before
# failing rather than hanging.
#
# If you deploy behind PgBouncer or a platform-managed pooler, point this at
# the pooler, add &pgbouncer=true, and size connection_limit to the pooler's
# per-client budget instead.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/kiwipilotprep?schema=public&connection_limit=10&pool_timeout=10"

# ---- Sessions --------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the session cookie. Rotating it signs everyone out, which is the
# correct emergency response to a suspected leak.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET="generate-a-32-byte-random-secret"

# ---- Site ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The canonical origin, no trailing slash. Used for canonical URLs, Open Graph
# tags, robots.txt and the sitemap. Set this in production or those will all
# point at localhost.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://kiwipilotprep.co.nz"

# ---- File storage ----------------------------------------------------------
# Two drivers, chosen by whether S3_BUCKET is set below.
#
# Disk (the default). Where uploaded guarantee-claim documents are written.
# Must be OUTSIDE the web root and outside the repository, on a disk that is
# included in backups.
MEDIA_DIR="./.dev/media"

# Bucket. Set S3_BUCKET and the app stores and serves every file — lesson
# diagrams, question images, thumbnails, claim documents — from an
# S3-compatible bucket instead of the disk, and MEDIA_DIR is ignored.
#
# REQUIRED on any host without a persistent disk. On a serverless platform the
# filesystem is read-only apart from a per-invocation /tmp, so the disk driver
# cannot store an upload at all and cannot read back one written by an earlier
# deploy: every diagram 404s and every claim upload fails.
#
# Keep the bucket PRIVATE — no public access, no custom domain. Files are only
# ever served through the app's own authenticated routes, which check
# entitlement and verify a signed token first.
#
# For Cloudflare R2 the endpoint is https://<ACCOUNT_ID>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# and the region is "auto" (R2 requires one and ignores its value). Credentials
# come from an R2 API token scoped to this one bucket.
#
# Move existing files across with: npm run media:push
# (it is resumable and preserves storage keys, so nothing in Postgres changes)
#S3_BUCKET="kiwipilotprep-media"
#S3_ENDPOINT="https://your_account_id.r2.cloudflarestorage.com"
#S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID="your_r2_access_key_id"
#S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="your_r2_secret_access_key"
#S3_REGION="auto"

# ---- Payments (Razorpay) ---------------------------------------------------
# Leave all three unset to run without a live gateway. Test keys start rzp_test_,
# live keys rzp_live_. The secret and webhook secret are server-side only and
# must never appear in client code, a build log, or a screenshot.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID="rzp_test_your_key_id_here"
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET="your_key_secret_here"
# From the Razorpay dashboard when you register the webhook endpoint at
# POST /api/webhooks/razorpay. Signatures are verified against this.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Left empty, the app falls back to a sandbox secret
# derived from AUTH_SECRET — fine locally, but then a genuine Razorpay
# webhook is signed with a secret the app does not know, every delivery is
# rejected as a forgery, and paid orders are never settled by the webhook.
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# The built-in sandbox gateway: signs and settles orders locally so checkout,
# entitlements and refunds can be exercised end to end without a real account.
# It is opt-in on purpose rather than keyed off NODE_ENV, because a production
# build also runs locally. MUST be absent or "false" in production.
#ALLOW_SANDBOX_PAYMENTS="true"

# ---- Email (Resend) --------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Signup does not complete without it: a new account#
# must confirm its address before it can start a free trial or buy anything,
# and password reset is delivered the same way.
#
# Leave RESEND_API_KEY empty to log outgoing mail to the server console instead
# of sending it — useful locally, and safe: nothing leaves the machine. Server
# side only; it must never reach the browser.
RESEND_API_KEY="re_your_key_here"

# The sender. Its DOMAIN must be verified in the Resend dashboard, using the
# DNS records Resend gives you, before delivery is reliable. See
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md for what to check.
EMAIL_FROM="KiwiPilotPrep <no-reply@kiwipilotprep.co.nz>"

# Automated tests read verification and password-reset links from the server
# console, because the tokens themselves are stored only as hashes. Set this
# ONLY for a test run. It is refused outright when NODE_ENV=production.
# EMAIL_DEV_LOG_LINKS="1"

# ---- Seeding ---------------------------------------------------------------
# prisma/seed.ts creates demo accounts with published passwords and refuses to
# run against a non-local DATABASE_URL. Set this only for a disposable
# database you are certain about. Never set it in production.
# ALLOW_DEMO_SEED="1"
